{"id": "aa5c193dcbe9e385:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:d32fd3251dbb5a91", "source_id": "aa5c193dcbe9e385", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-accounts/tax-advantaged-accounts/retirement-savings/403b-and-457b-plans", "topic": "403(b) and 457(b) Plans", "subtopic": "403(b) and 457(b) Plans", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "These pre-tax contributions and their investment earnings will not be taxed until you withdraw the money, typically after you retire.", "question": null, "answer": "403(b) and 457(b) plans are tax-deferred retirement savings programs that certain employers may offer. You can contribute pre-tax money from your paycheck and invest it in options provided by the plan. The contributions and their investment earnings generally are not taxed until you withdraw the money. Some employers offer both types of plans and allow contributions to each, but availability depends on the employer.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1134, "output_tokens": 203}}
{"id": "aa5c193dcbe9e385:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:d32fd3251dbb5a91", "source_id": "aa5c193dcbe9e385", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-accounts/tax-advantaged-accounts/retirement-savings/403b-and-457b-plans", "topic": "403(b) and 457(b) Plans", "subtopic": "403(b) and 457(b) Plans", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "These pre-tax contributions and their investment earnings will not be taxed until you withdraw the money, typically after you retire.", "question": "When are contributions and investment earnings in a 403(b) or 457(b) plan generally taxed?", "answer": "The contributions are made with pre-tax money, and the contributions and investment earnings generally are not taxed until you withdraw the money, typically after retirement.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1134, "output_tokens": 159}}
{"id": "aa5c193dcbe9e385:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:d32fd3251dbb5a91", "source_id": "aa5c193dcbe9e385", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-accounts/tax-advantaged-accounts/retirement-savings/403b-and-457b-plans", "topic": "403(b) and 457(b) Plans", "subtopic": "403(b) and 457(b) Plans", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "Some employers offer both 403(b) and 457(b) plans and allow you to contribute to both plans.", "question": "Misconception: If your employer offers both a 403(b) and a 457(b), you must choose just one plan to contribute to.", "answer": "Not necessarily. Some employers offer both plans and allow employees to contribute to both. Check with your employer to learn which plans are available to you.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1134, "output_tokens": 205}}
{"id": "aa5c193dcbe9e385:tutorial:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:6e18e63b74ca4992", "source_id": "aa5c193dcbe9e385", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-accounts/tax-advantaged-accounts/retirement-savings/403b-and-457b-plans", "topic": "403(b) and 457(b) Plans", "subtopic": "403(b) vs. 457(b) Employer Eligibility", "content_type": "tutorial", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Employers such as public educational institutions (public schools, colleges, and universities), certain non-profits, and churches or church-related organizations may offer 403(b) plans. Employers such as state and local government agencies and certain non-profit organizations may offer 457(b) plans.", "question": null, "answer": "Employer eligibility is one difference between 403(b) and 457(b) plans. Public educational institutions, certain nonprofits, and churches or church-related organizations may offer 403(b) plans. State and local government agencies and certain nonprofits may offer 457(b) plans. Some employers offer both plans, so check with your employer to learn which are available to you.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1136, "output_tokens": 219}}
{"id": "aa5c193dcbe9e385:qa:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:6e18e63b74ca4992", "source_id": "aa5c193dcbe9e385", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-accounts/tax-advantaged-accounts/retirement-savings/403b-and-457b-plans", "topic": "403(b) and 457(b) Plans", "subtopic": "403(b) vs. 457(b) Employer Eligibility", "content_type": "qa", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "directly_supported", "source_evidence": "Employers such as public educational institutions (public schools, colleges, and universities), certain non-profits, and churches or church-related organizations may offer 403(b) plans.  Employers such as state and local government agencies and certain non-profit organizations may offer 457(b) plans.", "question": "What types of employers may offer 403(b) plans, and what types may offer 457(b) plans?", "answer": "Public educational institutions, certain nonprofits, and churches or church-related organizations may offer 403(b) plans. State and local government agencies and certain nonprofits may offer 457(b) plans. Some employers offer both.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1136, "output_tokens": 212}}
{"id": "aa5c193dcbe9e385:misconception:gpt-6-luna::subtopic:6e18e63b74ca4992", "source_id": "aa5c193dcbe9e385", "source_url": "https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-accounts/tax-advantaged-accounts/retirement-savings/403b-and-457b-plans", "topic": "403(b) and 457(b) Plans", "subtopic": "403(b) vs. 457(b) Employer Eligibility", "content_type": "misconception", "teacher_model": "gpt-6-luna", "generation_status": "generated", "skip_reason": null, "grounding": "derived_from_source", "source_evidence": "Employers such as public educational institutions (public schools, colleges, and universities), certain non-profits, and churches or church-related organizations may offer 403(b) plans. Employers such as state and local government agencies and certain non-profit organizations may offer 457(b) plans.", "question": "Misconception: Only public educational institutions can offer 403(b) plans, and only state or local governments can offer 457(b) plans. What is the correction?", "answer": "The source describes broader eligibility: certain non-profits and churches or church-related organizations may offer 403(b) plans, while certain non-profit organizations may offer 457(b) plans. The listed employer types are not limited to schools for 403(b) plans or government agencies for 457(b) plans.", "formula_latex": null, "source_evidence_exact_match": true, "review_status": "unverified", "api_usage": {"input_tokens": 1136, "output_tokens": 304}}
